  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a drug-triggered disassembly mechanism for a vaccine-carrying supramolecular material. On the left, many blue GyrB-containing units are linked together into a dense network, with dark curved connectors labeled PEG helping form the larger assembly. Green vaccine particles are trapped or embedded within this packed structure.\n\nAn arrow marked “+ Novobiocin” points to the right, where the network has broken apart into smaller, separate rosette-like clusters. At the same time, the vaccine components are no longer confined inside the matrix and appear dispersed among the fragments. Small gray and dark dumbbell-like molecules, labeled novobiocin and coumermycin, are shown around the released particles, indicating that these small molecules regulate the assembly state.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a controllable delivery system: a crosslinked GyrB/PEG scaffold can hold vaccine cargo in an aggregated form, and addition of novobiocin shifts the balance toward dissociation into smaller units, enabling cargo release. The main visual comparison is therefore between a compact, loaded network before treatment and a dispersed, release-ready state after drug addition.",
